-- Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two, six trucks, cars and hover vehicles (printed 215-224): Rhino, Road King, Salamander, Smokeskipper, Urban Patrol Hovercar, Wolftrack
-- 6 vehicle rows, with 64 locations and 7 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~325-northern-gun-2-trucks-b.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-bd7-rhino-bulldozer', 'NG-BD7 Rhino Military Bulldozer', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'One.', '50 mph (80 km) maximum on most ground, grassland, sandy desert and rough terrain included. Woodland, very thick brush, mud and deep water are likely impassable without a well-worn path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km).', NULL, 'Fords shallow water to 2 ft (0.6 m) deep at most.', 'Height 9 ft (2.7 m); width 12 ft (3.7 m); length 26 ft (7.9 m)', '11 tons', 300, 1, NULL, 550000, '550,000 credits electric battery or liquid fuel, 700,000 Solid Oxide, 920,000 nuclear (12 year life).', 'Model NG-BD7 (Class: Armored Military Bulldozer/Engineering Vehicle). Not the Triax XM-350 Rhino. A tracked, heavily armoured dozer for combat engineering: it drags off wrecked tanks and armoured vehicles, which robots with hands do poorly, salvages, clears roads and fields, mends defenses, and shoves through rubble, M.D. razor wire and concrete dragon''s-teeth tank traps. It breaches walls so infantry can get in, and its raised blade serves as moving cover for troops advancing behind it. Losing one track cuts speed by 90% and costs -40% piloting. With the windshield shot out the driver is exposed, though usually in EBA and a Called Shot target at -6. With the Main Body gone the machine dies, its electronics are ruined, and the doors keep 1D4x10 M.D.C. until that is shot away. Civilian bulldozers have a third of these M.D.C. figures throughout. Penalties: none on most ground; -10% piloting on sandy desert and ice and through thick brush, light forest, mud or snow; -20% over mountains, in mud over 5 inches (12.7 cm) deep, and in water or snow over 2 ft (0.6 m). Clearing a way through a forest or debris field is slow work, unlike driving across one, and medium or large trees must be felled before the dozer can haul them off. Range: 400 miles (640 km) on gasoline, 1,000 miles (1,600 km) per multi-battery electric charge, unlimited on Solid Oxide or nuclear. Cargo: the blade pushes up to 4 tons and the recovery crane tows up to 30; a glove box holds a pistol or the like, with room behind the seats and straps outside for bigger loads. Sensors: a basic radio plus heating and cooling, nothing more.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.215-216'),
  ('ng-road-king-armored-suv', 'Road King Armored SUV', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Five; folding the rear seats down for cargo leaves room for only one.', 'Up to 120 mph (192 km) on pavement, dirt tracks or stony desert; at most 70 mph (112 km) on grassland, sandy desert, broken roads and rough ground. Woodland and thick brush are likely impassable without a wide, well-worn path, and even then under 20 mph (32 km).', NULL, 'Fords water, mud or snow up to 3 ft (0.9 m) deep (-10% piloting).', 'Height 12 ft (3.7 m); width 10 ft (3 m); length 22 ft (6.7 m)', '4 tons', 140, 1, NULL, 43000, '43,000 credits gasoline engine, 72,000 advanced multi-battery electric, 445,000 Solid Oxide, 875,000 nuclear (10 year life).', 'Vehicle Type: All-Terrain Utility Vehicle. An armoured sport-utility take on the Big Boss, sharing its chassis and much of its engine design, so parts and modifications carry over; it seats six and adds a long enclosed rear for supplies. The roof carries a built-in rack, which many owners swap for a gunner''s nest: a sliding roof hatch lets a passenger work a turret weapon if one is fitted. Losing a tire cuts speed by 35% and costs -25% driving, and riding on the rim can do more harm; losing two strands it until they are replaced. With the Main Body gone it is wrecked beyond repair. Penalties: none on pavement, short grass, dirt roads and stony desert; -5% piloting over gravel or tall grass; -10% over broken roads or sand and in water, mud or snow to 3 ft (0.9 m). Deeper water, mud and snow, forest and dense growth are likely impassable, though it crosses sand dunes. Range: 300 miles (480 km) on gasoline, 1,000 miles (1,600 km) per charge on the advanced multi-battery electric system (half that with the old battery), unlimited on Solid Oxide or nuclear. Cargo: 1,200 lbs (540 kg) with passengers aboard; seats up, there is room behind them for a rifle, canteens and three backpacks, and more can be lashed to the running boards, back and roof at the risk of shaking loose. It tows 6 tons, and a standard electric winch pulls 6 tons to free itself or drag a trailer, load or other vehicle. Weapons and sensors: none standard; up to four hovercycle weapon systems can be fitted.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.217'),
  ('ng-hch-1020-salamander', 'NG-HCH-1020 Salamander Amphibious Heavy Cargo Hauler', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two: a pilot and a co-pilot or communications engineer.', 'One in the cab between the crew seats, and two more squeezed in behind them for short trips; pods carry more by type: the RV pod houses 4-6 in comfort, a Sardine Can travel pod 32 (48 on the floor).', 'Hover only: 75 mph (120 km) maximum; 45 mph (72 km) once the cargo passes the figure the page prints as 120,000 tons total (each pod holds up to 40 tons). A fourth pod, or most alternative containers, costs 10% of speed; one big NG hovertrain boxcar, the most it can take, costs 50%.', 'Cannot fly; hovers 6 ft (1.8 m) up at most, usually 3-4 ft (0.9-1.2 m) when hauling heavy cargo or a wheeled container.', 'Cannot ride on the surface, but crosses ponds, marsh and streams up to 8 ft (2.4 m) deep. With every pod airtight it submerges and runs along the bed of a lake, river or sea at 25 mph (40 km/21.7 knots), to 1.2 miles (1.9 km) deep; it cannot surface like a submarine, so it needs a beach or slope to climb out.', 'Height 16 ft (4.9 m) with a standard pod; width 22 ft (6.7 m) for a standard pod or flatbed, taking hovertrain boxcars and wide loads up to 48 ft (14.6 m); length 24 ft (7.3 m) for the cab plus 30 ft (9.1 m) per pod', '32 tons empty: 14 for the cab and 6 per pod, cargo not included', 412, 1, NULL, 15000000, '15 million credits for the cab with its front and back main thrusters; pods are bought separately: Sardine Can travel pod 1-1.5 million, cargo pod 200,000, cattle car 500,000, field lab 3-8 million, flatbed 200,000, fuel/water pod 1 million, prison pod 725,000, refrigerated cargo car 350,000, RV motor home 350,000. Sold only at Ishpeming and MercTown, with poor availability; special orders take 1D4 months.', 'Model NG-HCH-1020 (Class: Amphibious Heavy Cargo Hauler and Exploration Vehicle). A privately owned miniature hovertrain, smaller and cheaper than the Big Bertha and built more for exploring than hauling: an enclosed cab and, as standard, three modular hover pods, each with its own linking hover system (hence even a plain pod''s price). Buyers mix the pods to suit: a merchant or mercenary outfit might run all cargo or travel pods, explorers an RV pod nearest the cab, then a lab or cargo pod, then cargo or a flatbed for big, slow robots and vehicles. It can trade its pods for hovertrain boxcars, flatbeds or APTs. Trim panels top and bottom make the long rig look like a commercial truck or motor home. Unlike a hovertrain it needs no terminal or ship: it travels the whole route itself, and goes under lakes, rivers and seas to dodge monsters, bandits or storms. A fourth pod makes it unstable: -10% speed and an extra -15% piloting, -35% when dodging or stunt driving. Each main thruster lost cuts speed 20% and costs -5% piloting, cumulatively; with all four gone it still creeps along at -80% speed and no more than 2 ft (0.6 m) up. With the cab''s Main Body gone it shuts down and is useless. Cargo: up to 120 tons safely at a fair speed; the cab holds an overhead gun rack and a locker about the size of a walk-in closet. Piloting: -10% for a vehicle of 100-130+ ft (30.5-40 m), -15% for a quick stop, -30% for a sharp turn, evasion, dodging or stunts, all cumulative; it crosses most land but not forest without a path. Power: nuclear only, about 10 years. Weapons: none, since many towns bar armed combat vehicles, a gun makes the driver look hostile and the cab and thrusters into targets, and insurers will not pay for raids on an armed rig; valuable loads travel under escort. An owner may still fit two light or medium weapons, on the roof and on either cab side. Sensors: those of a sealed commercial environmental vehicle, with life support and air for occupied compartments, a radio reaching 50 miles (80 km), and hidden cameras at the cab front and sides, the nose and the tail of the last pod so the driver can see with the window plates shut. Pods: Sardine Can travel pod, with a shared washroom (four toilets, four urinals, four sinks, but no shower), a little meeting room, and 10-16 sleeping cabins, each of the usual sixteen holding two fold-down bunks, a shallow closet and a bench, for 32 travellers or 48 on the floor; built to move 2-5 squads, travellers or refugees. Cargo pod, a sealable air- and watertight boxcar that is not environmental, so animals inside suffocate in 3D4x10 minutes. Cattle car, with pens and racks and basic life support. Field lab, equipped much like the Behemoth Explorer''s labs, with life support and running water. Flatbed. Fuel/water pod for liquids, not environmental. Prison pod, with life support and 16 caged cells of 200 M.D.C. each for prisoners, slaves or exotic beasts. Refrigerated cargo car, chilling or freezing on several settings. RV motor home, with one private bedroom, a bunk room for six (8-10 cramped), a kitchenette, a washroom with a shower, and a lounge whose narrow table serves for meals or work, two small couches and a bench.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.219-220'),
  ('ng-f911-smokeskipper', 'NG-F911 Smokeskipper Hover Firetruck', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One pilot/driver, plus four gunners outside at the water cannons.', 'Four firefighters or passengers up front, and four human-sized or two giant riders on the running boards along each side; the running boards slide open to take two stretcher patients per side.', 'Cannot drive; it skims as low as 1 ft (0.3 m) at full speed.', 'Hovers, VTOL, or flies at up to 120 mph (192 km), cruising at 50 mph (80 km); ceiling 200 ft (61 m). Range effectively unlimited on nuclear, though the hover jets must cool after 8 hours of continuous running; gas, diesel and electric models go about 200 miles (320 km) per tank or charge.', 'None.', 'Height 14 ft (4.3 m), the cherry-picker reaching another 50 ft (15.2 m) up or sideways; width 12 ft (3.7 m); length 25 ft (7.6 m)', '7 tons fully loaded; 15.3 tons with a full water tank', 190, 1, NULL, 2900000, '2.9 million credits electric or gas/diesel, 3.4 million Solid Oxide, 4.5 million nuclear (15 year life).', 'Model NG-F911 (Class: Armored Hover Vehicle), a hovering firetruck. Northern Gun''s hover know-how and military design put to fighting fires: one big water cannon in the main turret, two small hose cannons in rear turrets, and a third on an extending cherry-picker atop the truck that lifts victims off balconies and out of windows. Besides its own tank it can draw from a hydrant, water tower, lake or river through extending hoses that pull water in themselves, with no pressure needed at the source. Side panels open on lockers of medical gear, oxygen and more firefighting supplies, so it doubles as an ambulance. Vampire hunters have taken to it nearly as keenly as fire crews, using the cherry-picker as a mobile turret to hose down a lair or a pack of Wild Vampires, and NG now ships more of them to its Southwest dealers. With the Main Body gone it shuts down and is useless. Cargo: a 6x4x4 ft interior bay besides the passenger room (the page prints its metric depth as 12 m); its tank takes 2,000 gallons (7,571 liters) of water or fire-suppressing chemicals, which weigh 8.3 tons. Sensors: not a military suite, but an infrared imager on the passenger side of the cab lets the crew read a blaze''s heat and spot coming flare-ups and blow-outs.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.221-222'),
  ('ng-hc28-urban-patrol-hovercar', 'NG-HC28 Urban Patrol Hovercar', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', 'Up to four, three of whom can be held in the separated back seat.', 'Hover only: from a stationary hover to 120 mph (192 km), 1-10 ft (0.3-3 m) off the ground.', 'Cannot truly fly; hovers no higher than 10 ft (3 m).', 'None.', 'Height 5.6 ft (1.7 m); width 7 ft (2.1 m); length 14 ft (4.3 m)', '1.5 tons', 140, 1, NULL, 412000, '412,000 credits electric, 620,000 Solid Oxide, 980,000 nuclear (12 year life).', 'Model NG-HC28 (Class: Hover Capable Police Squad Car). A cheap, tough patrol car for the many small police forces, sheriffs, militias and town watches across North America that cannot get matching equipment, from a team led by Rob Daily, Mike Pointer and Kristopher Main. It carries two light weapons for self-defense and the usual police fittings: a computer and radio networking the squad cars with each other and headquarters, and a dashboard camera that logs chases and stops, which headquarters can watch live or review to make charges stick. A 20 M.D.C. plexiglass screen walls off the reinforced back seat, where built-in 3 M.D.C. straps pin a prisoner''s arms and legs, good for holding Juicers, Crazies and strong D-Bees. Common in Northern Gun and its neighbours among town police and militias, while the mercenaries policing Ishpeming and Escanaba prefer hovercycles and military machines; Black Marketeers, bandits, raiders and slavers use them too, for the guns and to pass as police. Each main hover jet lost cuts speed by 25% and costs -5% driving. With the Main Body gone it is wrecked beyond repair. Cargo: room up front for two energy rifles; the trunk''s first section holds two spare Urban Warrior or Huntsman armors, two billy clubs, two neural maces, a shotgun with 20 rounds, two tear gas and two smoke grenades and ten heavy cable ties for prisoners; the second holds 12 road flares, three reflective traffic cones, two first aid kits, a spring-loaded window breaker, an E-Clip powered jaws of life and 150 ft (46 m) of caution tape. Sensors: as a robot vehicle''s, plus flashing police lights and a siren in the front grille, and a dashboard camera with a 500 ft (152 m) range holding 72 hours of video before it must be offloaded.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.222-223'),
  ('ng-epc-18-wolftrack', 'NG-EPC-18 Wolftrack Exploration Personnel Carrier', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two: a pilot and a co-pilot or communications officer.', 'Eight; a dozen more (three dozen standing) can fit in the cargo area, other rooms and outside for short trips.', '75 mph (120 km) maximum on most ground, grassland, rocky and sandy desert and most rough terrain included; half that on ice and in mud over 3 ft (0.9 m) deep. Woodland and deep water and mud are likely impassable without a well-worn path, and even then under 10 mph (16 km).', NULL, 'Drives through water up to 17 ft (5.2 m) deep, with only the tops of its two exhaust stacks clear; 20 mph (32 km) once the water reaches 8 ft (2.4 m) or more.', 'Height 19 ft 7 in (6 m); width 20 ft (6.1 m); length 32 ft (9.8 m)', '28.7 tons fully loaded', 370, 1, NULL, 15000000, '15 million credits Solid Oxide, 16 million nuclear (12 year life), new and fully loaded. Replacement wheels are 35,000 credits each. Optional weapons cost extra.', 'Model NG-EPC-18 Exploration Personnel Carrier (Class: Scientific Transport Vehicle and Military Personnel Carrier). From designers Scott Kendrick, David Rogers and Eric Eisenmenger: a huge environmental wilderness truck, a part-amphibious mobile lab and field base for explorers, scientists, researchers and VIPs, and a favourite of adventurers, scouts, miners, border guards, mercenaries and outlaws. Inside are a field laboratory, a kitchen, a small meeting room, bunks for up to twelve, and a washroom (two toilets and two urinals, two sinks, one small shower). Heavy plate armor covers it, along with many lights and driving cameras. Four solid M.D.C. wheels, each 10 ft (3 m) across and 6 ft (1.8 m) wide, lift the belly 5 ft (1.5 m) clear, and independent suspension carries it over rough ground, debris and rock fields. A heavy crane holds a trailer, hauls the Wolftrack itself out of trouble, or pulls smaller vehicles from mud, water, snow or ice. Losing a wheel cuts speed by 35% and costs -25% driving, and riding on the rim can do more harm; losing two strands it until they are replaced. With the Main Body gone it is wrecked beyond repair. Penalties: none on most ground; -15% piloting on sandy desert, mud, ice and snow; -20% over mountains and in mud over 4 ft (1.2 m) or snow over 8 ft (2.4 m). Range: unlimited. Cargo: storage throughout the housing plus an 8x12x9 ft (2.4 x 3.7 x 2.7 m) cargo bay; it tows a trailer of up to 32 tons. Power: nuclear or Solid Oxide only. Weapons: none standard; at extra cost up to eight hovercycle weapon systems, or four of the heavy vehicle weapons listed under the Pathmaker, usually on the roof or sides toward the front or back. Sensors: as a robot vehicle''s.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.223-224');

